  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatTri_3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "At your appointment, your doctor discusses a phase III trial with you.
    They explain that this trial is testing whether a new drug works better than
    the standard treatment already in use. Later, a friend expresses concern and
    says, \u201CIf you join a phase III trial, you might only get a placebo and no
    treatment at all.\u201D That leaves you worried \u2014 would participating really
    mean missing out on treatment?"
  explanation: In cancer clinical trials, patients are never left untreated. Even
    if a placebo is used, it is given along with the standard treatment, so everyone
    receives care. In this case, the trial compares whether adding the new treatment
    provides better results than the current standard alone.
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Trials
